"Predictive optimization for Unity Catalog managed tables | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization)\nciteturn45view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 193\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Skip to main content L3: \nL4: [Button: On this page]\nL5: \nL6: Last updated on Sep 11, 2026\nL7: # Predictive optimization for Unity Catalog managed tables\nL8: \nL9: Predictive optimization automatically runs `OPTIMIZE`, `VACUUM`, and `ANALYZE` on Unity Catalog managed tables (Delta Lake and Iceberg) on Databricks, eliminating manual maintenance and time spent tracking performance issues.\nL10: \nL11: note\nL12: Predictive optimization is enabled by default for accounts created on or after November 11, 2024. Databricks is enabling existing accounts with a gradual rollout. This rollout is expected to complete by August 2026. To check whether your account is already enabled, see cite2†Verify whether predictive optimization is enabled .\nL13: \nL14: With predictive optimization enabled, Databricks automatically does the following:\nL15:   * Identifies tables that would benefit from maintenance operations and queues those operations to run.\nL16:   * Collects statistics when data is written to a managed table.\nL17: \nL18: This eliminates unnecessary maintenance runs and the burden of tracking and troubleshooting performance manually.\nL19: Databricks recommends predictive optimization for all Unity Catalog managed tables. For example, automatic liquid clustering uses intelligent optimization of data layout based on your data usage patterns. See cite3†Use liquid clustering for tables .\nL20: ## What operations does predictive optimization run?cite4†​ L21: \nL22: Predictive optimization runs the following operations on Unity Catalog managed tables:\nL23: Operation  | Description\nL24: --- | ---\nL25: `OPTIMIZE`  | Triggers incremental clustering for enabled tables. See cite3†Use liquid clustering for tables . Improves query performance by optimizing file sizes. See cite5†Optimize data file layout .\nL26: `VACUUM`  | Reduces storage costs by deleting data files no longer referenced by the table. On tables with Iceberg reads enabled, also cleans up Iceberg metadata for older table versions. See cite6†Remove unused data files with vacuum and cite7†VACUUM and Iceberg metadata cleanup .\nL27: `ANALYZE`  | Scans the table and collects statistics to improve query performance. See cite8†ANALYZE TABLE … COMPUTE STATISTICS . To remove statistics collected by predictive optimization, see cite9†ANALYZE TABLE … DROP STATISTICS .\nL28: [Button: ←][Button: ✕]\nL29: Operation  | Description\nL30: --- | ---\nL31: `OPTIMIZE`  | Triggers incremental clustering for enabled tables. See cite3†Use liquid clustering for tables . Improves query performance by optimizing file sizes. See cite5†Optimize data file layout .\nL32: `VACUUM`  | Reduces storage costs by deleting data files no longer referenced by the table. On tables with Iceberg reads enabled, also cleans up Iceberg metadata for older table versions. See cite6†Remove unused data files with vacuum and cite7†VACUUM and Iceberg metadata cleanup .\nL33: `ANALYZE`  | Scans the table and collects statistics to improve query performance. See cite8†ANALYZE TABLE … COMPUTE STATISTICS . To remove statistics collected by predictive optimization, see cite9†ANALYZE TABLE … DROP STATISTICS .\nL34: note\nL35: \nL36: `OPTIMIZE` does not run `ZORDER` when executed by predictive optimization. On tables that use Z-order, predictive optimization ignores Z-ordered files.\nL37: \nL38: If automatic liquid clustering is enabled, predictive optimization might select new clustering keys before clustering data. See cite10†Automatic liquid clustering .\nL39: \nL40: warning\nL41: The retention window for `VACUUM` is determined by the `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` table property, which defaults to 7 days. `VACUUM` removes data files no longer referenced by a Delta table version within that window. To retain data for longer durations (for example, to support extended time travel), set this property before you enable predictive optimization:\nL42: \nL43: SQL\nL44: \nL45:     `ALTER TABLE table_name SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration' = '30 days');\nL46:     `\nL47: If you set `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` to less than 7 days, predictive optimization still retains data files for a minimum of 7 days when it runs `VACUUM FULL`. This 7-day minimum prevents data loss and preserves recent time travel for the table.\nL48: ## Compute and billingcite11†​ L49: \nL50: Predictive optimization runs `ANALYZE`, `OPTIMIZE`, and `VACUUM` operations using serverless compute for jobs. Your account is billed for this compute using a serverless jobs SKU.\nL51: \nL52: See pricing for cite12†Databricks managed services†www.databricks.com . See cite13†Track predictive optimization with system tables .\nL53: ## Prerequisitescite14†​ L54: \nL55: The following requirements must be met to use predictive optimization:\nL56: \nL57:   * Your Databricks workspace must be on the cite15†Premium plan or above†databricks.com in a cite16†supported region .\nL58:   * You must use SQL warehouses or Databricks Runtime 12.2 LTS or above.\nL59:   * Only Unity Catalog managed tables are supported.\nL60: ## Enable predictive optimizationcite17†​ L61: \nL62: You can enable predictive optimization for an account, a catalog, a schema, or a table. All Unity Catalog managed tables inherit the account value by default. You can override the account default at the catalog, schema, or table level.\nL63: \nL64: You must have the following privileges to enable or disable predictive optimization:\nL65: Unity Catalog object  | Privilege\nL66: --- | ---\nL67: Account  | Account admin\nL68: Catalog  | Catalog owner, or a user with `MANAGE` privilege on the catalog\nL69: Schema  | Schema owner, or a user with `MANAGE` privilege on the schema\nL70: Table  | Table owner, or a user with `MANAGE` privilege on the table\nL71: \nL72: [Button: ←][Button: ✕]\nL73: Unity Catalog object  | Privilege\nL74: --- | ---\nL75: Account  | Account admin\nL76: Catalog  | Catalog owner, or a user with `MANAGE` privilege on the catalog\nL77: Schema  | Schema owner, or a user with `MANAGE` privilege on the schema\nL78: Table  | Table owner, or a user with `MANAGE` privilege on the table\nL79: ### Enable or disable predictive optimization for your accountcite18†​ L80: \nL81: An account admin can enable predictive optimization for all metastores in an account. Catalogs and schemas inherit this setting by default, but you can override it at either level.\nL82: \nL83:   1. Go to the accounts console.\nL84:   2. Navigate to Settings, then Feature enablement.\nL85:   3. Select the option you want (for example, Enabled) next to Predictive optimization.\nL86: \nL87: note\nL88:   * Metastores in regions that don't support predictive optimization aren't enabled.\nL89:   * Disabling predictive optimization at the account level does not disable it for catalogs or schemas that have specifically enabled it.\nL90: ### Enable or disable predictive optimization for a catalog, schema, or tablecite19†​ L91: \nL92: Predictive optimization uses an inheritance model. When enabled for a catalog, schemas in that catalog inherit the setting, and tables within an enabled schema inherit it as well. You can explicitly enable or disable predictive optimization for a catalog, schema, or table to override this behavior.\nL93: \nL94: note\nL95: You can disable predictive optimization at the catalog, schema, or table level before enabling it at the account level. If predictive optimization is later enabled at the account level, it remains blocked for objects that have specifically disabled it.\nL96: \nL97: Use the following syntax to enable, disable, or reset predictive optimization to inherit from the parent object:\nL98: SQL\nL99: \nL100:     `ALTER CATALOG [catalog_name] { ENABLE | DISABLE | INHERIT } PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\nL101:     ALTER { SCHEMA | DATABASE } schema_name { ENABLE | DISABLE | INHERIT } PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\nL102:     ALTER TABLE table_name { ENABLE | DISABLE | INHERIT } PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\nL103:     `\nL104: \nL105: See cite20†ALTER TABLE .\nL106: ## Verify whether predictive optimization is enabledcite21†​ L107: \nL108: The `Predictive Optimization` field is a Unity Catalog property that shows whether predictive optimization is enabled. If the setting is inherited from a parent object, the field value indicates this.\nL109: \nL110: Use the following syntax to check the status:\nL111: \nL112: SQL\nL113: \nL114:     `DESCRIBE (CATALOG | SCHEMA | TABLE) EXTENDED name\nL115:     `\nL116: ## Check why predictive optimization skipped a tablecite22†​ L117: \nL118: On Databricks Runtime 18 LTS and above, after predictive optimization evaluates a managed table, you can check why an operation was skipped in two ways:\nL119: \nL120:   * Query the table with `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED ... AS JSON`.\nL121:   * Use the History tab in Catalog Explorer.\nL122: \nL123: Results might take up to 24 hours to appear.\nL124: ### Use `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED ... AS JSON`cite23†​ L125: \nL126: `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED ... AS JSON` returns a `predictive_optimization_evaluations` field in the output. This field lists each operation type and the most recent evaluation result for it.\nL127: \nL128: To check evaluation results for a table, run the following:\nL129: \nL130: SQL\nL131: \nL132:     `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED catalog_name.schema_name.table_name AS JSON\nL133:     `\nL134: The `predictive_optimization_evaluations` field shows the most recent result for each operation type. Only the latest evaluation is shown with no history. Operation types include:\nL135: \nL136:   * `COMPACTION`\nL137:   * `CLUSTERING`\nL138:   * `AUTO_CLUSTERING_COLUMN_SELECTION`\nL139:   * `VACUUM`\nL140: \nL141: The evaluation output does not show skip reasons for `ANALYZE` operations.\nL142: ### Use the history tab in Catalog Explorercite24†​ L143: \nL144: You can view skip reasons in the History tab for a table in Catalog Explorer. In the Operation column, the Auto label indicates the operation ran, and the Not applied label indicates the operation was skipped. The Auto label includes predictive optimization and other automatic operations, such as streaming auto-compaction.\nL145: To filter which operations appear, use the dropdown in the History tab. Select All runs to show every operation, Automatic runs to show only automatic operations such as predictive optimization, or Manual runs to show only operations that were triggered manually. To find skipped predictive optimization operations, select Automatic runs, then look for rows with the Not applied label.\nL146: cite25†Image: History tab with the run type dropdown expanded, showing All runs, Automatic runs, and Manual runs, and a skipped VACUUM operation labeled Not applied L147: \nL148: To view the skip reason, click a row with the Not applied label in the Operation column.\nL149: \nL150: cite26†Image: Catalog Explorer history tab showing predictive optimization skip reasons L151: \nL152: The following table shows how the operation types in the `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED` output map to operations shown in Catalog Explorer:\nL153: `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED` operation type  | Catalog Explorer operation  | Description\nL154: --- | --- | ---\nL155: `COMPACTION`  | `OPTIMIZE`  | File compaction to improve query performance\nL156: `CLUSTERING`  | `OPTIMIZE`  | Incremental liquid clustering\nL157: `AUTO_CLUSTERING_COLUMN_SELECTION`  | `AUTO LIQUID`  | Evaluation or evolution of liquid clustering keys\nL158: `VACUUM`  | `VACUUM`  | Removal of data files no longer referenced by the table\nL159: \nL160: [Button: ←][Button: ✕]\nL161: `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED` operation type  | Catalog Explorer operation  | Description\nL162: --- | --- | ---\nL163: `COMPACTION`  | `OPTIMIZE`  | File compaction to improve query performance\nL164: `CLUSTERING`  | `OPTIMIZE`  | Incremental liquid clustering\nL165: `AUTO_CLUSTERING_COLUMN_SELECTION`  | `AUTO LIQUID`  | Evaluation or evolution of liquid clustering keys\nL166: `VACUUM`  | `VACUUM`  | Removal of data files no longer referenced by the table\nL167: ## Track predictive optimization with system tablescite27†​ L168: \nL169: Databricks provides the system table `system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history` for observability into predictive optimization operations, costs, and impact. See cite28†Predictive optimization system table reference .\nL170: ## Limitationscite29†​ L171: \nL172: Predictive optimization does not run on the following table types:\nL173: \nL174:   * Tables loaded to a workspace as OpenSharing recipients\nL175:   * External tables\nL176: \nL177: On this page\nL178:   * cite4†What operations does predictive optimization run? L179:   * cite11†Compute and billing L180:   * cite14†Prerequisites L181:   * cite17†Enable predictive optimization L182:     * cite18†Enable or disable predictive optimization for your account L183:     * cite19†Enable or disable predictive optimization for a catalog, schema, or table L184:   * cite21†Verify whether predictive optimization is enabled L185:   * cite22†Check why predictive optimization skipped a table L186:     * cite23†Use `DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED ... AS JSON` L187:     * cite24†Use the history tab in Catalog Explorer L188:   * cite27†Track predictive optimization with system tables L189:   * cite29†Limitations L190: cite30†Privacy Notice†www.databricks.com ·cite31†Terms of Use†www.databricks.com ·cite32†Modern Slavery Statement†www.databricks.com ·cite33†California Privacy†www.databricks.com ·Your Privacy Choices cite34†Image†www.databricks.com L191: \nL192: © Databricks 2026. All rights reserved. Apache, Apache Spark, Spark and the Spark logo are trademarks of the cite35†Apache Software Foundation†www.apache.org .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is Photon? | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/photon)\nciteturn45view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/photon\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 112\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Skip to main content L3: \nL4: [Button: On this page]\nL5: \nL6: Last updated on Sep 11, 2026\nL7: # What is Photon?\nL8: \nL9: Photon is the Databricks-native vectorized query engine that accelerates your SQL workloads, DataFrame API calls, ETL pipelines, and stateless streaming workloads. Photon processes data in columnar batches, delivering significant performance improvements over traditional row-based execution. Photon is also compatible with Apache Spark APIs, so it works with your existing code with no changes required.\nL10: ## How Photon workscite2†​ L11: \nL12: For supported operations, Photon replaces the JVM-based Spark SQL execution engine with a native C++ runtime. The Apache Spark query optimizer (Catalyst) still plans your query, but Photon takes over at the execution layer, processing data in columnar batches rather than row by row. When Photon encounters an unsupported operation during query execution, it transparently falls back to the Spark runtime for the remainder of that operation.\nL13: Photon processes data in batches of thousands of rows at a time, enabling modern CPUs to use SIMD instructions that evaluate multiple values per CPU cycle. By executing in native C++ instead of the JVM, Photon eliminates garbage collection pauses, JIT warm-up delays, and memory overhead. The columnar batch processing enables cache-friendly sequential reads, which maximizes memory bandwidth and CPU pipeline efficiency.\nL14: \nL15: Photon's architecture improves performance in a number of ways:\nL16:   * Query acceleration: Photon provides up to 5x better price/performance for data and analytics workloads compared to other cloud data warehouses, as measured by industry-standard TPC-DS benchmarks.\nL17:   * Optimized joins and shuffles: Replaces sort-merge joins with high-performance hash joins and uses a redesigned columnar shuffle to increase throughput for large-scale joins.\nL18:   * Write performance: The Photon native Parquet writer accelerates Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, and Parquet writes, including `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE INTO`, `INSERT`, and `CREATE TABLE AS SELECT` operations. Wide tables with thousands of columns see especially significant improvements.\nL19:   * Scan efficiency: Implements filter pushdown, dictionary pruning, and row-group skipping to reduce data read from storage, even when handling many small files.\nL20:   * Disk cache and concurrency: Provides faster repeat access via the disk cache and improves throughput for concurrent queries in interactive BI workloads.\nL21:   * Integration with SQL and Dataframes API: Supports SQL and DataFrame APIs across Python, R, Scala, and Java with no code changes required.\nL22: Photon provides the greatest benefit for longer-running queries that process large datasets. Queries that normally complete in under two seconds don't see meaningful improvement because execution time is dominated by planning and scheduling overhead rather than data processing.\nL23: ## Integration with the Databricks platformcite3†​ L24: \nL25: Photon accelerates workloads across the Databricks platform. You don't need to change your code or queries to take advantage of Photon.\nL26:   * SQL analytics and BI: Photon is the default engine for all SQL warehouses, powering dashboards, ad hoc queries, and scheduled reports.\nL27:   * ETL and data engineering: Batch jobs built with SQL or the DataFrame API benefit from faster scans, joins, aggregations, and writes. The native Parquet writer is particularly effective for ingestion into Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, or Parquet tables.\nL28:   * Lakeflow pipelines: Enabling Photon in your pipeline configuration helps to accelerate cite4†Lakeflow pipelines execution.\nL29:   * Streaming: Photon supports stateless streaming when writing to a Delta or Parquet sink. Supported sources include Delta, Parquet, CSV, JSON, Kafka, and Kinesis. Stateful streaming is not supported.\nL30:   * AI and machine learning: Photon improves performance for Spark SQL, DataFrames, feature engineering, and GraphFrames operations.\nL31: ## Photon enablementcite5†​ L32: \nL33: Photon is enabled on serverless compute, SQL warehouses, and serverless Lakeflow pipelines.\nL34: For classic all-purpose compute, jobs compute, and classic Lakeflow pipelines, Databricks enables Photon by default. Turn it on or off with the Use Photon Acceleration checkbox under Performance when creating or editing compute. See cite6†Use Photon acceleration . If you create these resources using the cite7†Clusters API or cite8†Jobs API , you must explicitly enable Photon by setting `runtime_engine` to `PHOTON`. If you use the cite9†Pipelines API , set `photon` to `true`.\nL35: ### Features that require Photon enablementcite10†​ L36: \nL37: The following features require Photon enablement:\nL38: \nL39:   * Predictive I/O for read and write. See cite11†What is predictive I/O? .\nL40:   * Dynamic file pruning in `MERGE`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` statements. See cite12†Dynamic file pruning .\nL41: ### Supported instance typescite13†​ L42: \nL43: Photon supports a number of instance types on the driver and worker nodes, including Graviton-based instances. Photon instance types consume DBUs at a different rate than the same instance type running the non-Photon runtime. For more information about Photon instances and DBU consumption, see the cite14†Databricks pricing page†databricks.com .\nL44: ## Supported operators, expressions, and data typescite15†​ L45: \nL46: Photon covers the following operators, expressions, and data types. When a query uses an unsupported operation, Photon transparently falls back to the Spark runtime for that portion of the execution.\nL47: \nL48: Operators\nL49:   * Scan (Parquet, Delta, CSV, JSON), Filter, Project\nL50:   * Hash Aggregate/Join/Shuffle\nL51:   * Nested-Loop Join\nL52:   * Null-Aware Anti Join\nL53:   * Spatial Join (broadcast and shuffled variants supporting `ST_Intersects`, `ST_Contains`, `ST_Covers`, `ST_Equals`, `ST_Touches`, `ST_Within`, and `ST_DWithin`)\nL54:   * Union, Expand, ScalarSubquery\nL55:   * Delta/Parquet Write Sink\nL56:   * Sort, TopK, Limit\nL57:   * Window Function\nL58: \nL59: Expressions\nL60: These categories are representative, not exhaustive. Individual functions within each category may have limitations.\nL61: \nL62:   * Comparison / Logic\nL63:   * Arithmetic / Math\nL64:   * Conditional (IF, CASE, etc.)\nL65:   * String\nL66:   * Casts\nL67:   * Aggregates, including Min/Max/MinBy/MaxBy on nested types\nL68:   * Date/Timestamp/DateFormat\nL69: \nL70: Data types\nL71:   * Byte/Short/Int/Long\nL72:   * Boolean\nL73:   * String/Binary\nL74:   * Decimal\nL75:   * Float/Double\nL76:   * Date/Timestamp\nL77:   * TimestampNTZ\nL78:   * Struct\nL79:   * Array\nL80:   * Map\nL81:   * Variant\nL82:   * Null\nL83:   * cite16†Geometry L84:   * cite17†Geography L85:   * Collated string\nL86: ## Monitor Photon usagecite18†​ L87: \nL88: You can monitor how much of your query runs on Photon using the following tools:\nL89:   * Spark UI (classic all-purpose and jobs compute): In the SQL/DataFrame tab of the cite19†Spark UI , Photon operators appear in orange in the query DAG visualization. Standard Spark operators appear in blue. This helps you identify which parts of your query benefit from Photon and which fall back to the Spark runtime.\nL90:   * Query profile (SQL warehouses and serverless compute): The Execution Details view shows the percentage of task time spent in Photon. The query plan distinguishes Photon operators (purple) from standard operators (grey).\nL91: If you notice that a query isn't using Photon as expected, check whether the query uses unsupported operations, UDFs, or data formats that cause a fallback to the Spark runtime.\nL92: ## Limitationscite20†​ L93: \nL94:   * If your workload hits an unsupported operation, the compute resource transparently switches to the Spark runtime for the remainder of that operation. Your query still produces correct results.\nL95:   * Photon doesn't support UDFs (User Defined Functions), RDD APIs, or Dataset APIs.\nL96:   * Stateful streaming is not supported. Photon supports stateless streaming only.\nL97:   * Photon doesn't improve queries that normally run in under two seconds.\nL98:   * Graviton-based instances with Photon don't support cite21†Databricks Container Services for dedicated compute or Databricks SQL warehouses. This limitation doesn't apply to cite22†Databricks Container Services for standard compute .\nL99: \nL100: On this page\nL101:   * cite2†How Photon works L102:   * cite3†Integration with the Databricks platform L103:   * cite5†Photon enablement L104:     * cite10†Features that require Photon enablement L105:     * cite13†Supported instance types L106:   * cite15†Supported operators, expressions, and data types L107:   * cite18†Monitor Photon usage L108:   * cite20†Limitations L109: cite23†Privacy Notice†www.databricks.com ·cite24†Terms of Use†www.databricks.com ·cite25†Modern Slavery Statement†www.databricks.com ·cite26†California Privacy†www.databricks.com ·Your Privacy Choices cite27†Image†www.databricks.com L110: \nL111: © Databricks 2026. All rights reserved. Apache, Apache Spark, Spark and the Spark logo are trademarks of the cite28†Apache Software Foundation†www.apache.org .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnable workload identity federation for GitHub Actions | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/auth/provider-github)\nciteturn45view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/auth/provider-github\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 144\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Skip to main content L3: \nL4: [Button: On this page]\nL5: \nL6: Last updated on Oct 2, 2026\nL7: # Enable workload identity federation for GitHub Actions\nL8: \nL9: Databricks OAuth token federation, also known as OpenID Connect (OIDC), allows your automated workloads running outside of Databricks to securely access Databricks without Databricks secrets. See cite2†Authenticate access to Databricks using OAuth token federation .\nL10: \nL11: To enable workload identity federation for GitHub Actions:\nL12: \nL13:   1. cite3†Create a federation policy L14:   2. cite4†Configure the GitHub Actions YAML file L15: After you enable workload identity federation, the Databricks SDKs and the Databricks CLI automatically fetch workload identity tokens from GitHub and exchange them for Databricks OAuth tokens.\nL16: ## Create a federation policycite5†​ L17: \nL18: First, create a workload identity federation policy. For instructions, see cite6†Configure a service principal federation policy . For GitHub, set the following values for the policy:\nL19:   * Organization: The name of your GitHub organization. For example, if your repository URL is `https://github.com/databricks-inc/data-platform`, then the organization is `databricks-inc`.\nL20:   * Repository: The name of the single repository to allow, such as `data-platform`.\nL21:   * Entity type: The kind of GitHub entity represented in the `sub` (subject) claim of your token. The default is Branch. Databricks recommends using Environment, which you can enable by setting the `environment` attribute in your GitHub Actions YAML file. See cite7†Deploying to a specific environment†docs.github.com .\nL22:   * Issuer URL: `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`\nL23:   * Subject: A string formed by concatenating values from the GitHub Actions job context. The required format depends on your repository's GitHub `sub` claim. See the following note.\nL24:   * Audiences: Databricks recommends setting this to your Databricks account ID. If omitted, the account ID is used by default.\nL25:   * Subject claim: (Optional) The JWT claim that contains the workload identity (`sub`) value from the OIDC token. For GitHub, leave the field as `sub`, which encodes the repository, branch, tag, pull/merge request, or environment that triggered the workflow. To authenticate as a reusable workflow rather than the calling repository, see cite8†Authenticate using a reusable workflow .\nL26: note\nL27: \nL28: Databricks matches the Subject against the token's `sub` claim exactly, and GitHub's `sub` format depends on when the repository was created. Repositories created, renamed, or transferred on or after July 15, 2026 include numeric organization and repository IDs, such as `repo:octo-org@123456/octo-repo@456789:environment:prod`. Earlier repositories use `repo:<org>/<repo>:environment:prod`, unless they opt in to the immutable format.\nL29: To find your repository's exact `sub`, see cite9†About security hardening with OpenID Connect†docs.github.com . The immutable format isn't available on GitHub Enterprise Server.\nL30: \nL31: For example, the following cite10†Databricks CLI command creates a federation policy for an organization named `my-org` and a Databricks service principal numeric ID of `5581763342009999`. The example subject uses the name-only format for a repository created before July 15, 2026:\nL32: Bash\nL33: \nL34:     `databricks account service-principal-federation-policy create 5581763342009999 --json '{\nL35:       \"oidc_policy\": {\nL36:     \t\"issuer\": \"https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com\",\nL37:     \t\"audiences\": [\nL38:       \t  \"a2222dd9-33f6-455z-8888-999fbbd77900\"\nL39:     \t],\nL40:     \t\"subject\": \"repo:my-github-org/my-repo:environment:prod\"\nL41:       }\nL42:     }'\nL43:     `\nL44: ## Configure the GitHub Actions YAML filecite11†​ L45: \nL46: Next, configure the GitHub Actions YAML file. Set the following environment variables:\nL47: \nL48:   * `DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE`: `github-oidc`\nL49:   * `DATABRICKS_HOST`: Your Databricks workspace URL\nL50:   * `DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID`: The service principal (application) ID\nL51: YAML\nL52: \nL53:     `name: GitHub Actions Demo\nL54:     run-name: ${{ github.actor }} is testing out GitHub Actions 🚀\nL55:     on: workflow_dispatch\nL56:     permissions:\nL57:       id-token: write\nL58:       contents: read\nL59:     jobs:\nL60:       my_script_using_wif:\nL61:         runs-on: ubuntu-latest\nL62:         environment: prod\nL63:         env:\nL64:           DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE: github-oidc\nL65:           DATABRICKS_HOST: https://my-workspace.cloud.databricks.com/\nL66:           DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID: a1b2c3d4-ee42-1eet-1337-f00b44r\nL67:         steps:\nL68:           - name: Checkout repository\nL69:             uses: actions/checkout@v4\nL70:           - name: Install Databricks CLI\nL71:             uses: databricks/setup-cli@main\nL72:           - name: Run Databricks CLI commands\nL73:             run: databricks current-user me\nL74:     `\nL75: ## Authenticate using a reusable workflowcite12†​ L76: \nL77: By default, the `sub` claim identifies the calling repository. To authenticate as a reusable workflow rather than the calling repository, set `subject_claim` to `job_workflow_ref` in the federation policy. Any team can invoke the reusable workflow, but only the reusable workflow itself authenticates with Databricks.\nL78: ### Create a federation policycite13†​ L79: \nL80: Create a federation policy using `job_workflow_ref` as the subject claim. Set `subject` to the ref of your reusable workflow file:\nL81: Bash\nL82: \nL83:     `databricks account service-principal-federation-policy create 5581763342009999 --json '{\nL84:       \"oidc_policy\": {\nL85:         \"issuer\": \"https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com\",\nL86:         \"audiences\": [\nL87:           \"a2222dd9-33f6-455z-8888-999fbbd77900\"\nL88:         ],\nL89:         \"subject\": \"my-github-org/shared-workflows/.github/workflows/deploy.yml@refs/heads/main\",\nL90:         \"subject_claim\": \"job_workflow_ref\"\nL91:       }\nL92:     }'\nL93:     `\nL94: ### Configure the GitHub Actions YAML filescite14†​ L95: \nL96: Create a reusable workflow that authenticates with Databricks, and a calling workflow in any repository that invokes it.\nL97: \nL98: The following example shows a reusable workflow file (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` in the shared workflows repository):\nL99: YAML\nL100: \nL101:     `on:\nL102:       workflow_call:\nL103:     jobs:\nL104:       deploy:\nL105:         runs-on: ubuntu-latest\nL106:         env:\nL107:           DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE: github-oidc\nL108:           DATABRICKS_HOST: https://my-workspace.cloud.databricks.com/\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeclarative Automation Bundles deployment modes | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes)\nciteturn45view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 91\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nManage data quality with pipeline expectations | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations)\nciteturn45view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 295\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nbundle command group | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands)\nciteturn45view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1392\n"
